    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 6824.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 777,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it is so important.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The States of Water\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and use for rain (found in oceans, rivers, and lakes).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F), found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F), found invisibly in the air as humidity or visibly as steam.\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can carry vital nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through our bodies and through the soil.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze solid from the bottom up, destroying all aquatic life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our body temperatures stable.\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Coverage:** About **71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in the oceans.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite the Earth looking blue and wet, **97% of Earth's water is saltwater** (found in oceans and un-drinkable without desalination). \n*   **Freshwater:** Only about **3% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in polar ice caps, glaciers, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and ponds.\n\n### 4. Why Water Matters for Life\nWater is the essential building block of all known life. \n*   **Biology:** The human body is made up of about 60% water. Our brains are roughly 73% water, our lungs 83%, and even our bones are about 31% water. \n*   **Survival:** A human can survive for weeks without food, but only a few days without water. It regulates our internal temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and transports oxygen through our blood.\n*   **Ecosystems:** Every plant, animal, and microorganism relies on water to carry out metabolic processes, grow, and reproduce.\n\n**In short:** Water is the lifeblood of planet Earth—a simple molecule that creates, sustains, and shapes the world around us."
